From 28223339f71d8dffc3eaab3e9e3e64f1cfd2bab8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 04:50:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 072/104] packetizer: mpegaudio: let a break cost one frame,
 not the rest of the block

The packetizer takes one frame per call and hands what is left of the block
back to its caller, which offers it again on the next call. A block that
arrived carrying a discontinuity therefore arrives carrying it every time,
and each time the parser is reset and its bytestream emptied. The second
reset finds the block's timestamp already spent - it is cleared once used, so
that no two frames claim it - so the remainder is dropped for want of one and
the parser waits for the next block to be dated.

A block off a transport or program stream holds three or four mpeg audio
frames, so a hundred milliseconds of the pass went that way at every loop of
a repeat, with the first frames of the item among them. Clear the flag once
it has been acted on: the break belongs to the seam the block arrived on, not
to the rest of the block, which is continuous with itself.

Measured on a 4 s item looped with --repeat on a null sink, silence per loop
in the capture, ts and mpeg-ps carrying mp2:

    ts        0.315 / 0.505 / 0.505 s  ->  0.220 / 0.250 / 0.255 s
    mpeg-ps   0.475 / 0.665 / 0.655 s  ->  0.370 / 0.560 / 0.555 s

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 modules/packetizer/mpegaudio.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/modules/packetizer/mpegaudio.c b/modules/packetizer/mpegaudio.c
index 0769b9a5fc..3e201ac001 100644
--- a/modules/packetizer/mpegaudio.c
+++ b/modules/packetizer/mpegaudio.c
@@ -307,6 +307,14 @@ static block_t *DecodeBlock( decoder_t *p_dec, block_t **pp_block )
                 block_Release( p_block );
                 return NULL;
             }
+
+            /* What is left of this block once a frame has been taken out of
+             * it comes straight back here, and the break belongs to the seam
+             * the block arrived on, not to the rest of the block: leaving the
+             * flag set makes every following frame reset the parser again,
+             * and the second reset arrives with the timestamp already spent,
+             * so the remainder of the block is dropped for want of one. */
+            p_block->i_flags &= ~BLOCK_FLAG_DISCONTINUITY;
         }
 
         if( !date_Get( &p_sys->end_date ) && p_block->i_pts <= VLC_TICK_INVALID )
